course: dictionary
lesson: 2067
title: Consolidation: making things firmer or joining them together
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Consolidation is a noun, and it has a few meanings. The main idea is making something stronger, firmer or more settled, or joining several things into one bigger thing. A «consolidation exercise» helps you practise what you have learned, so it stays firm in your memory.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «Consolidation of learning» is about school and strengthening what you know. «Consolidation of two schools» is about joining, so it means bringing things together. The other words in the sentence tell you which meaning fits.
explain: In formal language about money and maths, consolidation means putting several debts or accounts together. The example «"Fiscal consolidation" means cuts in public spending and tax rises» shows this formal use. You do not need to know every word in it to see that it is about money.
fact: Consolidation is a noun.
fact: «Consolidation» means making something stronger, firmer or more settled.
fact: «Consolidation» can also mean joining several things into one bigger thing.
fact: In formal money and maths language, consolidation means putting several debts or accounts together.
fact: «Fiscal consolidation» means cuts in public spending and tax rises.
exercise: «Practising spellings every night is a useful consolidation of what you learned.» What does consolidation mean here? | *Making what you learned stronger and firmer | Forgetting a lesson quickly | Going on a long trip | Breaking things into small pieces
exercise: The two small schools had a consolidation, so they became one bigger school. What does consolidation mean here? | Painting the classrooms | Making a school smaller | *Joining several things into one bigger thing | A quiet test
exercise: «"Fiscal consolidation" means cuts in public spending and tax rises.» Which kind of language is this? | Animal names | *Formal money language | Playground games | Music words
test: Which phrase means making learning stronger? | *A consolidation exercise | A broken lesson | A lost pencil
test: Putting several debts together is an example of what? | *Consolidation | Erosion | Sunshine
exam: What does consolidation mean in «consolidation of two schools»? | *Joining two things into one bigger thing | Losing the school bell | Making the school smaller | Splitting one school into many
